alerts.action_en:
1) On the database host run systemctl status mssql-server (Linux) or Get-Service MSSQLSERVER (Windows) to check the service;
2) If the service is running, connect by hand with sqlcmd -S <host>,1433 -U <user> to separate a network problem, a port not listening, and a locked account;
3) If the service is down, check /var/opt/mssql/log/errorlog (Linux) or the SQL Server error log; a full disk, tempdb creation failure, or an expired certificate are common causes;
4) After recovery run SELECT name, state_desc FROM sys.databases to confirm every database is back ONLINE.

1) Run SELECT name, state_desc FROM sys.databases WHERE state_desc <> 'ONLINE' to identify the database;
2) Look for the most recent errors for that database in the SQL Server error log to tell an IO error from log corruption;
3) Restoring from the most recent good backup (RESTORE DATABASE) is the path with the least data loss and should be preferred;
4) Only if no backup exists should you use EMERGENCY mode plus DBCC CHECKDB(..., REPAIR_ALLOW_DATA_LOSS); that operation loses data, so take a file-level snapshot first.

1) Run SELECT name, state_desc FROM sys.databases WHERE state_desc = 'OFFLINE' to list the offline databases;
2) Confirm against the change record whether this is planned maintenance or a migration;
3) If unplanned, try ALTER DATABASE [db] SET ONLINE and read the error log for the exact reason if it fails;
4) Once online run DBCC CHECKDB([db]) WITH NO_INFOMSGS to confirm data consistency.

1) Take volume_mount_point from the alert labels and run SELECT DB_NAME(database_id), name, size*8/1024 AS size_mb FROM sys.master_files ORDER BY size DESC to find the largest files;
2) For an oversized log file, run BACKUP LOG to truncate it first and then DBCC SHRINKFILE; under the full recovery model it cannot shrink without a log backup;
3) If the data files genuinely need that space, clean up historical partitions or archive cold data;
4) As an emergency measure move tempdb or backup files to another volume, but plan a disk expansion.

1) Take instance (the database name) from the alert labels and run SELECT name, log_reuse_wait_desc FROM sys.databases WHERE name = '<db>' to see why the log cannot be reused;
2) For LOG_BACKUP, run BACKUP LOG [db] TO DISK='...' immediately; this is by far the most common cause;
3) For ACTIVE_TRANSACTION, run DBCC OPENTRAN to find the long transaction and decide whether to KILL it;
4) For REPLICATION or AVAILABILITY_REPLICA, investigate whether replication/AlwaysOn synchronisation is stuck and repair the replication link first.

1) Run SELECT * FROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters WHERE counter_name LIKE '%Page life%' to confirm the live value;
2) Run SELECT TOP 20 * FROM sys.dm_exec_query_stats ORDER BY total_logical_reads DESC to find the statements with the highest logical reads, usually full scans caused by missing indexes;
3) Take the suggestions from sys.dm_db_missing_index_details, review them, and create the indexes; this pays off faster than adding memory;
4) If queries are already optimised and it is still low, check whether max server memory is set too small or is being squeezed by other processes before adding RAM.

1) Run SELECT session_id, requested_memory_kb, granted_memory_kb, wait_time_ms FROM sys.dm_exec_query_memory_grants to see who is waiting and who is holding;
2) For sessions with huge grants, pull the statement with sys.dm_exec_sql_text; it is usually a large sort or hash join, and a missing index amplifies the memory requirement significantly;
3) As an emergency measure KILL the largest consumer to release memory;
4) Longer term add the indexes suggested by dm_db_missing_index_details, split large queries, or raise max server memory.

1) Run SELECT blocking_session_id, session_id, wait_type, wait_time, wait_resource FROM sys.dm_exec_requests WHERE blocking_session_id <> 0 to find the head of the blocking chain;
2) For the head session use DBCC INPUTBUFFER(<spid>) or sys.dm_exec_sql_text to see what it is running;
3) Once confirmed as a runaway long transaction, run KILL <spid> to stop the bleeding;
4) Afterwards check whether that transaction lacks an index (widening the lock range) or the application forgot to commit, and consider enabling READ_COMMITTED_SNAPSHOT to reduce reader/writer conflict.

1) Query the system_health extended event session for the deadlock graph: SELECT XEvent.query('.') FROM (SELECT CAST(target_data AS XML) AS TargetData FROM sys.dm_xe_session_targets ...) and extract xml_deadlock_report;
2) Read the lock acquisition order and the indexes involved from the graph;
3) The usual fix is to make multi-table updates follow the same order everywhere in the application code, plus indexes on the participating queries to narrow the lock range;
4) In the short term the application can retry on error 1205, but that does not replace fixing the root cause.

1) Take database_name and logical_filename from the alert labels to identify the slow file;
2) On the host run iostat -x 1 (Linux) or watch Avg. Disk sec/Read in Performance Monitor to tell slow storage from read amplification inside SQL Server;
3) If the storage is fine, optimise queries and add indexes as for page life expectancy to reduce physical reads;
4) If the storage really is slow, consider moving the file to SSD, splitting filegroups to spread IO, or checking whether backup jobs overlap the business peak.

1) Take database_name and file_type from the alert labels and handle file_type=LOG first, since slow log writes stall every commit;
2) On the host run iostat -x 1 and check that disk's await and %util to see whether it is saturated;
3) If the log file shares a disk with the data files, move the log to a dedicated low-latency disk;
4) Check whether backups, DBCC CHECKDB, or index rebuilds overlap the business peak and move them to off-peak hours.
